   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>Maison de Famille</addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI_04">9, Rue de l’Université, 9</name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris (VII<hi rend="superscript">E</hi>)</name>
                            </addrLine>
                        </address>
                    </ab>
                </div> <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name>: I groped my way through a few pages of <hi rend="underline">Ithaca</hi> and found <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">the enclosed mistakes</seg>. Can you please see that they are added to your proof in their proper places? I hope the <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">copies of the <hi rend="underline">Evening</hi> <hi rend="underline">Standard</hi>
                        </seg> have gone off to the various addresses. If you have one left I should like to have it.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>With kindest regards</salute>                    	<salute>sincerely yours</salute>                               	<signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1922-09-29">29.ix.922</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">The enclosure with these corrections to the proofs of <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name>’s second edition, published on 12 October 1922 by Weaver’s Egoist Press, has been separated from the letter, but it is also kept at the British Library (BL 57356-10, 10v, 11; <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">JJA</name> 12: 182-84).</note> <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">Possibly the review “A Monstrous Book” in the London <hi rend="italic">Evening Standard</hi> from which Sisley Huddleston later quoted extensively in <hi rend="italic">
                        <name type="work">Paris Salons, Cafés, Studios</name>
                    </hi> (London: J. B. Lippincott, 1928), 208-20.</note> </div>
      </body>
